WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.575
Watch the blue profile. Changing just one boundary makes it bend, and its

00:00:05.575 --> 00:00:11.150
lowest eigenvalue rises. Why? Let us build the explanation,

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.105
then check the same effect in the laboratory.

00:00:15.492 --> 00:00:19.717
The horizontal coordinate is position along an extra dimension.

00:00:19.717 --> 00:00:24.617
The vertical coordinate is field amplitude, not a particle trajectory.

00:00:24.617 --> 00:00:29.847
With zero slope required at both ends, a constant profile is allowed.

00:00:29.847 --> 00:00:34.137
Its curvature, and its normal eigenvalue, are zero.

00:00:35.492 --> 00:00:39.117
Now turn on the boundary coupling, called a.

00:00:39.117 --> 00:00:43.807
The right edge requires slope plus a times amplitude to equal zero.

00:00:43.807 --> 00:00:48.157
Our nonzero constant has zero slope but nonzero amplitude.

00:00:48.157 --> 00:00:53.222
Watch the residual turn red. This profile no longer satisfies the boundary

00:00:53.222 --> 00:00:54.582
condition.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:58.885
We keep zero slope at the left edge.

00:00:58.885 --> 00:01:02.360
The bulk equation then gives a cosine profile.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.520
Change its frequency, x, and watch the right endpoint.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.530
Most frequencies still fail. Only the frequency that balances slope against

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.860
boundary amplitude is allowed.

00:01:15.242 --> 00:01:19.917
For a cosine, the endpoint slope is minus x sine x.

00:01:19.917 --> 00:01:22.877
The boundary term is a cosine x.

00:01:22.877 --> 00:01:26.867
At coupling one, these two contributions cancel.

00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:30.202
Rearranging gives x tangent x equals a.

00:01:30.202 --> 00:01:33.992
This equation comes directly from the endpoint balance.

00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:40.502
Increase the coupling. The allowed profile bends more, while the first root

00:01:40.502 --> 00:01:45.237
rises. The small graph tracks the same calculation.

00:01:45.237 --> 00:01:50.082
With interval length pi R, the scaled normal mass is x over pi.

00:01:50.082 --> 00:01:53.117
It approaches one half as the coupling grows.

00:01:54.492 --> 00:02:00.457
Now the actual laboratory. We change the boundary strength from zero to four.

00:02:00.457 --> 00:02:03.837
Watch the paired curve lift above the unpaired curve.

00:02:03.837 --> 00:02:09.642
At Wilson angle zero, its intercept is exactly the normal gap we just derived.

00:02:09.642 --> 00:02:13.397
The highlighted input and the plotted result change together.

00:02:14.783 --> 00:02:20.223
The boundary rule changed. The constant profile became inadmissible.

00:02:20.223 --> 00:02:24.073
The lowest allowed mode now has a positive eigenvalue.

00:02:24.073 --> 00:02:28.858
That is the mechanism behind this gap, in this stated interval problem.

00:02:28.858 --> 00:02:34.453
The laboratory computes the effect; the boundary argument explains why it happens.

00:02:35.825 --> 00:02:39.145
We have seen how a boundary selects a profile.

00:02:39.145 --> 00:02:44.320
Now ask a different question: how many profiles are independent? Start

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.435
with three simple two component functions.

00:02:47.435 --> 00:02:52.175
The first lives in the upper component, the second in the lower component, and the third

00:02:52.175 --> 00:02:56.525
changes with position. Watch their curves being drawn.

00:02:57.908 --> 00:03:01.858
Try subtracting half of the first profile from the third.

00:03:01.858 --> 00:03:05.013
Their upper components cancel at the midpoint.

00:03:05.013 --> 00:03:09.088
But move away from that point: the residual returns.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:14.028
Dependence would require one fixed set of coefficients to cancel the entire function,

00:03:14.028 --> 00:03:18.988
at every position. A cancellation at one point is not enough.

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:26.622
The Gram matrix records an overlap for each pair of profiles, integrated over the interval.

00:03:26.622 --> 00:03:31.917
Watch the entries appear. The first and third profiles overlap: their entry

00:03:31.917 --> 00:03:36.057
is one half. That does not make them dependent.

00:03:36.057 --> 00:03:40.982
The determinant is one twelfth, so this matrix is invertible and all three functions are

00:03:40.982 --> 00:03:42.442
independent.

00:03:43.825 --> 00:03:46.740
Use the same coefficients as before.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:50.060
The combined profile is u minus one half.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:56.190
Square its amplitude and integrate: the shaded area is one twelfth, not zero.

00:03:56.190 --> 00:04:01.230
This is exactly c star G c. In general it equals the squared norm of the

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:06.405
combined profile. A zero direction of G is therefore precisely a dependence

00:04:06.405 --> 00:04:07.745
relation.

00:04:09.117 --> 00:04:13.727
Now return to the three exponential profiles used in the laboratory.

00:04:13.727 --> 00:04:18.172
Each has two complex components and depends on two coordinates.

00:04:18.172 --> 00:04:22.027
The surface shows the first component of the middle profile.

00:04:22.027 --> 00:04:24.922
At mass parameter zero it is flat.

00:04:24.922 --> 00:04:29.107
As M increases, it gains a slope in the z direction.

00:04:29.107 --> 00:04:31.817
This new spatial dependence is the key.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.955
Suppose a fixed linear combination of these three profiles vanishes.

00:04:37.955 --> 00:04:40.570
Differentiate with respect to z.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:46.580
Only the middle profile changes, so for nonzero M its coefficient b must vanish.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:51.080
The two remaining spinor directions then force a and c to vanish.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:56.135
At M zero, that derivative argument disappears and the three constant profiles span

00:04:56.135 --> 00:04:58.150
only two directions.

00:04:59.533 --> 00:05:04.148
Change the profile mass from zero to one in the actual laboratory.

00:05:04.148 --> 00:05:08.523
At zero, one Gram eigenvalue is numerically zero.

00:05:08.523 --> 00:05:11.428
At one, all three are positive.

00:05:11.428 --> 00:05:16.913
Their sizes depend on normalization, and the numerical rank uses a tolerance.

00:05:16.913 --> 00:05:22.198
The independent rational interval certificate also proves positive definiteness at M one,

00:05:22.198 --> 00:05:26.898
with the smallest eigenvalue greater than zero point one one zero five.

00:05:28.283 --> 00:05:31.318
There is an important limit to this conclusion.

00:05:31.318 --> 00:05:37.468
Distinct exponential rates can produce four independent functions, or any finite number.

00:05:37.468 --> 00:05:42.813
Along a suitable line, their independence follows from a Vandermonde determinant.

00:05:42.813 --> 00:05:45.988
For the four displayed rates it is seventy two.

00:05:45.988 --> 00:05:50.998
A physical family count needs the actual operator domain and boundary conditions, not

00:05:50.998 --> 00:05:53.443
just three chosen profiles.

00:05:54.825 --> 00:05:59.550
Next, representation theory becomes an exact finite sum.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:04.475
For the positive half spin representation of Spin twenty, each weight is a list of ten

00:06:04.475 --> 00:06:09.770
signs, divided by two. Their product must be positive.

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:13.535
Choose nine signs freely; the tenth is fixed.

00:06:13.535 --> 00:06:18.895
Watch the allowed weights fill the grid: two to the ninth gives five hundred and twelve.

00:06:20.283 --> 00:06:25.453
At a Cartan probe, each weight contributes a number q, obtained by taking its dot

00:06:25.453 --> 00:06:30.498
product with the probe. For this example the insertion is the product of the first two

00:06:30.498 --> 00:06:35.028
signs. We sum the insertion times q squared.

00:06:35.028 --> 00:06:39.858
Set the first probe coordinates to one and two, with the rest zero.

00:06:39.858 --> 00:06:44.013
Four equal groups of weights give a total of five hundred and twelve.

00:06:45.367 --> 00:06:49.157
Expand the squared dot product before choosing a probe.

00:06:49.157 --> 00:06:53.347
There are fifty five quadratic monomials in ten variables.

00:06:53.347 --> 00:06:57.897
The checker sums each coefficient using exact rational arithmetic.

00:06:57.897 --> 00:07:03.087
Watch the cancellations: only the cross term h one times h two survives,

00:07:03.087 --> 00:07:06.447
with coefficient two hundred and fifty six.

00:07:06.447 --> 00:07:09.792
We have checked the complete quadratic polynomial.

00:07:11.158 --> 00:07:14.378
Now move the probe across that polynomial.

00:07:14.378 --> 00:07:17.858
On either coordinate axis the value is zero.

00:07:17.858 --> 00:07:21.968
Move to one, two and it becomes five hundred and twelve.

00:07:21.968 --> 00:07:28.443
The heat map makes the distinction visible: one zero evaluation cannot establish an identity.

00:07:28.443 --> 00:07:33.088
A nonzero evaluation can refute an identically zero polynomial.

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:38.950
The laboratory keeps its displayed ten dimensional Cartan probe fixed while we change

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:43.485
the insertion. Watch the exact trace table change.

00:07:43.485 --> 00:07:48.750
For the first insertion, the quadratic moment is five hundred and twelve, consistent

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:53.595
with our identity. Other insertions produce different moments.

00:07:53.595 --> 00:07:58.425
These are unweighted representation traces, before the normalization and inflow needed

00:07:58.425 --> 00:08:01.140
for physical anomaly coefficients.

00:08:02.492 --> 00:08:06.687
The finite weight sum answers a precise algebraic question.

00:08:06.687 --> 00:08:11.957
Turning it into an anomaly calculation requires the appropriate projectors, normalization

00:08:11.957 --> 00:08:16.287
and descent, together with the contributions of other sectors.

00:08:16.287 --> 00:08:19.562
Global charge data and inflow also matter.

00:08:19.562 --> 00:08:23.302
The moving marker stops at what we have actually computed.

00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:27.587
A trace certificate does not certify the complete quantum theory.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:33.825
Our final example is a restricted six dimensional consistency condition.

00:08:33.825 --> 00:08:37.810
Let N count the signed excess of complex Weyl doublets.

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:43.440
In the stated standalone SU two tensor setting, N must be divisible by six.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:48.520
With identical family assignments, three quark colours and an opposite lepton chirality

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:52.195
contribute three minus one: two per family.

00:08:52.195 --> 00:08:55.815
Watch each family advance the residue clock by two.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:01.440
After three such families, the clock returns to zero.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.070
Six and nine families also pass this condition.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:10.475
If the lepton chirality matches instead, each identical family contributes four;

00:09:10.475 --> 00:09:14.130
again, multiples of three return to zero.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:17.280
The congruence restricts the possibilities.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:20.590
It does not uniquely select three generations.

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:26.015
Now allow two families with different chirality assignments.

00:09:26.015 --> 00:09:29.575
The first contributes two, the second four.

00:09:29.575 --> 00:09:34.765
Together they make six, so this residue test passes with two families.

00:09:34.765 --> 00:09:39.355
This is a counterexample to dropping the identical assignment assumption.

00:09:39.355 --> 00:09:43.930
It is not a construction of a fully consistent two family physical model.

00:09:45.283 --> 00:09:48.803
Counting complex doublets gives modulus six.

00:09:48.803 --> 00:09:53.278
Counting in half doublet units doubles N and uses modulus twelve.

00:09:53.278 --> 00:09:57.413
Watch both clocks together: they pass at the same time.

00:09:57.413 --> 00:10:00.658
The units changed, not the condition.

00:10:00.658 --> 00:10:06.078
The physical argument also requires integral tensor quantization; factorizing an anomaly

00:10:06.078 --> 00:10:10.338
polynomial alone does not supply all that global information.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:16.655
Now change the family number in the laboratory, keeping the lepton chirality opposite

00:10:16.655 --> 00:10:22.740
to the quarks. One and two fail this restricted condition; three passes.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.360
Four and five fail; six passes again.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.745
The displayed residue explains every change.

00:10:29.745 --> 00:10:35.055
Keep the identical assignment and standalone theory assumptions attached to this result.

00:10:36.408 --> 00:10:39.353
We have followed four different questions.

00:10:39.353 --> 00:10:42.448
Boundary conditions selected a spectrum.

00:10:42.448 --> 00:10:46.163
Integrated overlaps tested function independence.

00:10:46.163 --> 00:10:48.958
Weight sums determined a polynomial.

00:10:48.958 --> 00:10:52.843
A residue condition constrained identical assignments.

00:10:52.843 --> 00:10:56.868
Each calculation has a reason, inputs and limits.

00:10:56.868 --> 00:11:01.933
Passing these separate diagnostics does not by itself determine a Higgs vacuum, neutrino

00:11:01.933 --> 00:11:05.588
masses or a complete Standard Model construction.

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:11.950
The companion page links the laboratory, ordinary proofs, exact certificates and their

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:17.300
checker. It also links the primary papers behind the assumptions.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:20.800
No proof assistant formalization is claimed.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:26.170
You can change the variables, reproduce the calculations, and challenge the missing physical

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:31.830
assumptions. The purpose is to make the path between physics and mathematics visible,

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:33.255
and checkable.
